      "title": "dfilter: Align with the C++11 and later definition of UCNs",
      "message": "dfilter: Align with the C++11 and later definition of UCNs\n\nIn C++, Universal Character Names (escapes of the form \\uNNNN or\n\\UNNNN) may not include members of the basic character set outside\nof character or string literals, but may do so inside character or\nstring literals. This makes expressions like \"\\u0077\" legal. WG14\nadopted N3124 sometime between the working drafts N3096 (shortly\nbefore C23) and N3220 (shortly afterwards), and we shall do so\nalso.\n\nWe only allow UCNs inside character and string literals, and it\ndoesn't make sense to restrict them as C does. We don't mention\nthe restriction in wireshark-filter man page (and didn't seem to\nmention UCNs in the WSUG section on display filters at all until\nnow) Switch to the easier to understand practice.\n\nhttps://en.cppreference.com/cpp/language/escape\nhttps://en.cppreference.com/c/language/escape\nhttps://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n3124.pdf\nhttps://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n3096.pdf\nhttps://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n3220.pdf\n",
      "author_name": "John Thacker",
      "author_email": "johnthacker@gmail.com",
      "authored_date": "2026-07-19T14:43:22.000-05:00",
      "committer_name": "John Thacker",
